<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monks Mound</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 950–1100</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monks Mound is the largest earthwork at Cahokia, near modern St Louis, and the largest prehistoric earthen structure in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Built in stages from about 950 to 1100, it rises in terraces to about 30 metres and covers some 6 hectares at its base. A large building, probably the residence or temple of Cahokia's leaders, stood on its summit. It is named after Trappist monks who lived nearby in the early 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monks-mound.jpg" alt="Monks Mound" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Skubasteve834 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monks_Mound_in_July.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
